/*!
    \page qtros2-urdfviewer-guide.html
    \title Generating a UI from a URDF file with urdfviewer
    \ingroup qtros2-guides

    \keyword urdf
    \keyword digital twin

    \brief How to generate a digital twin for your robot.

    \l {https://wiki.ros.org/urdf}{URDF} is an XML schema:
    Unified Robot Description Format. It's the industry-standard interchange
    format for vendors to describe everything that is needed to model robots:
    the list of links and joints, range of motion of each joint, meshes to
    render links realistically, visual properties of the materials, mass and
    inertial properties, collision volumes.

    If you purchase a robot for use with ROS, hopefully it comes with a URDF file.
    If you design your own, plugins are available for most CAD systems to
    export a URDF file.

    You can use the QtROS \c urdfviewer tool to generate a Qt Quick 3D scene
    containing a model of your robot, with bindings to subscribers for the most
    common telemetry topics. This is a good starting point for a digital twin,
    teach pendant or some other kind of UI that needs to show a live rendering
    of the robot's pose; you can then modify the generated code to add the rest
    of your desired UI.

    \c {urdfviewer --help} tells what arguments it accepts:

    \badcode
    $ urdfviewer --help
    Usage: urdfviewer [options] urdf or xacro destination
    Import a URDF file, preview and export a Qt Quick 3D scene

    Options:
      -h, --help                   Displays help on commandline options.
      --help-all                   Displays help, including generic Qt options.
      -v, --version                Displays version information.
      -b, --bridge                 Preview with live ROS bridge.
      -u, --upm <units>            Scene <units> per meter.
      -p, --topic-prefix <prefix>  DDS topic <prefix> for robot.

    Arguments:
      urdf or xacro                File to open
      destination                  Output directory
    \endcode

    \image urdfviewer-dogzilla.png

    After starting it and adjusting the settings as necessary,
    press the Run Preview button to generate the "live" 3D scene:
    if you are using standard topics like
    \c {/myrobot/body_pose/state} and \c {/myrobot/jointstates},
    you should see the model staying in sync with the robot's movements.

    Press the Run Export button to save the generated code to the
    directory shown in the Export Directory field.
*/
